> Great. I also have a leafletOutput below these, and I want it to be the same width as two "4" columns, but the height depends on the width. How can I do that?
**assistant**:
> To achieve a responsive `leafletOutput` that is the same width as two 4-column elements (which totals 8 columns), you can effectively wrap the `leafletOutput` in a `fluidRow` and `column`. Additionally, you can set the height of the `leafletOutput` dynamically using a reactive expression based on the input width.
> 
> Here’s an example of how to structure your UI to include the two `sliderTextInput` elements alongside a responsive `leafletOutput`:
> 
> ```r
> library(shiny)
> library(shinyWidgets)
> library(leaflet)
> 
> ui <- fluidPage(
>   # Define main panel
>   mainPanel(
>     fluidRow(
>       # Two Slider Text Inputs
>       column(6,
>              sliderTextInput(
>                inputId = "slider1",
>                label = "Slider 1",
>                choices = c("A", "B", "C"),
>                selected = "A"
>              )
>       ),
>       column(6,
>              sliderTextInput(
>                inputId = "slider2",
>                label = "Slider 2",
>                choices = c("D", "E", "F"),
>                selected = "D"
>              )
>       )
>     ),
>     # Leaflet Output that is 8 columns wide
>     fluidRow(
>       column(12,
>              leafletOutput("map", width = "100%", height = paste0(input$mapHeight, "px"))
>       )
>     )
>   )
> )
> 
> server <- function(input, output, session) {
>   # Example reactive height of the map based on user interaction if needed.
>   # For demonstration, you can set it to a fixed value or create a slider input.
>   output$mapHeight <- renderText({ 
>     # For instance, set height based on a calculation or input
>     400  # Adjust this value as needed
>   })
> 
>   output$map <- renderLeaflet({
>     leaflet() %>%
>       addTiles() %>%
>       setView(lng = 0, lat = 0, zoom = 2)  # Adjust map view as needed
>   })
> }
> 
> shinyApp(ui, server)
> ```
> 
> ### Key Points:
> 1. **Leaflet Width**: Set the `leafletOutput` width to `100%` to ensure it spans the entire column's width.
> 2. **Responsive Height**: You can adjust the height based on user inputs or set it to a fixed height initially. In this example, the height is set as a reactive value from `input$mapHeight`.
> 3. **Column Layout**: The leaflet is placed within a 12-column span, allowing it to occupy the full width below the sliders.
> 
> You can replace the logic inside `renderText` for `mapHeight` depending on how you want to dynamically set the height. Adjust this as needed for the functionality you desire!
